# D50 (2026-08-28): `unsafe` is REMOVED from the language. With the reserved
# keyword gone, `unsafe:` lexes as an identifier and lands on the NAMED SCOPE
# path — the reinterpretation the ruling accepts rather than diagnoses (adding
# a migration diagnostic would re-reserve the word the owner ruled must go).
#
# THIS FIXTURE IS THE DISCRIMINATOR, and its stdout ORDER is the whole point.
# The old `Stmt::Unsafe` arm called `lower_block_scoped`, which opens NO drop
# scope, so a local declared inside the block was dropped at FUNCTION exit.
# A named scope calls `lower_named_scope`, which pushes a drop scope and emits
# the drops at BLOCK exit. So this program's output changes:
#
#   pre-D50  : in block / after block / drop inner   <- drop LAST (function exit)
#   post-D50 : in block / drop inner / after block   <- drop at BLOCK exit
#
# RED-verified against the pre-removal compiler built from f3feea79, which
# printed the first ordering. This is also what makes
# `docs/language-reference.md`'s claim that the block "compiles identically to
# a normal block" TRUE — by removal, the promise the implementation never kept.
struct Resource:
    String name

equip Resource with Drop:
    void drop(!self):
        String n = self.name
        print(f"drop {n}")

void main():
    unsafe:
        Resource r = Resource("inner")
        print(f"in block {r.name}")
    print("after block")
